+## Subtopics
+
+### GASP Pipeline
+The specific implementation using flat Gaussians and black-box physics engines, avoiding meshing [S3, unofficial].
+
+### PhysGaussian
+Uses first-order approximation of deformation maps and linear transformations, requiring physics engine adjustments [S3, unofficial].
+
+### Gaussian Splashing
+Uses Position-Based Fluids for fluid simulation, building on GaussianShader for material parameters [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** The pipeline is described as "notably rapid" and does not increase rendering costs [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Efficiency:** Implementing a Gaussian hierarchy (simulating only selected Gaussians) significantly accelerates the process, especially with particle-based engines like Taichi [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Flexibility:** The method integrates with any black-box physics engine (e.g., Genesis, Blender, Taichi Elements) without modifying the engine or the GS model [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Comparison:** GASP avoids additional meshing strategies (like marching cubes) used by GSP, Embodied Gaussians, and VR-GS, operating directly on Gaussian components [S3, unofficial]. This contrasts with PhysGaussian, which uses a first-order approximation of the deformation map and requires adjustments to the physics engine [S3, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity**: Linear with respect to the number of Gaussians for conversion and reconstruction. Simulation cost depends on the physics engine (e.g., MPM complexity) [S3, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity**: Proportional to the number of Gaussians and triangle vertices [S3, unofficial].
+- **Performance**: Demonstrated superior performance on benchmark datasets for 3D object rendering [S3, unofficial].
+- **Strengths**: No additional meshing strategies required; compatible with any black-box physics engine; hierarchical grouping enhances efficiency [S3, unofficial].
+- **Limitations**: Artifacts from discrete point treatment if not managed; requires flat Gaussian initialization [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-*   **Origin:** The GASP (Gaussian Splatting for Physic-Based Simulations) pipeline was introduced by Waczja et al. [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Development:** It builds upon the GaMeS framework, which uses flat Gaussians [S3, unofficial]. The method was demonstrated using the Genesis engine, Blender software, and the Taichi Elements library [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Context:** It addresses the need for physically accurate dynamics in 3DGS, which is often used for animation [S1][S2, unofficial]. Previous methods like PhysGaussian adapted Newtonian dynamics via linear transformations, while GASP uses a direct mapping to triangle faces [S3, unofficial].
+Originating from the GASP paper (2024), this method addresses the need for physically accurate dynamics in 3DGS [S3, unofficial]. It builds on GaMeS (flat Gaussian parameterization) [S3, unofficial] and contrasts with PhysGaussian (2023) which uses linear approximations [S3, unofficial]. It is demonstrated using the Genesis engine, Blender, and Taichi Elements [S3, unofficial].
 
